    "result_text": "Done. The full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec called for membership **level 4 to require tenure of more than 365 days**, so a brand-new owner (zero tenure) never exceeds level 3.\n\n**The problem:** `Owner.getMembershipLevel()` in `src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java` computed a level from pre-tenure factors (start at 1, +1 for an email, +1 for `namesakeCount == 0`) and then hard-capped it at 3 (`Math.min(level, 3)`) \u2014 level 4 was documented as \"reserved for tenure\" but was never actually reachable, and there was no tenure logic at all.\n\n**The change** (`Owner.java:216`):\n- Kept the pre-tenure factors capped at 3.\n- Added a tenure gate: `+1` when `registrationDate` is set and the whole days from `registrationDate` to today exceed 365, then capped the result at 4.\n- Since a newly created owner's `registrationDate` is today, its tenure is 0, so it stays at \u22643 \u2014 exactly matching the example (email + `namesakeCount` 0 + 3-member household \u2192 level 3, not 4). Household member count was never a factor in the formula; it's just descriptive context in the example.\n\nI also updated the two Javadoc/doc descriptions to match (`Owner.java` and `openapi.yml`, raising the schema `maximum` from 3 to 4).\n\n**Verification:** The existing acceptance test `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` (asserts a new emailed, unique-named owner returns `membershipLevel: 3`) passes, and the full `./mvnw test` suite reports `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
